# Shared route table, imported by both deploy/Caddyfile (hosted, TLS) and
# deploy/Caddyfile.local (local dev, plain HTTP) so the two never drift apart.
#
# ONE flat /api prefix. Two things to know before changing this:
#
# 1. DO NOT drop /api entirely. Each origin here serves BOTH the Next.js UI
#    and the API (see deploy/Caddyfile: `import api_routes` then a catch-all
#    `handle` to the web container). vendor-web has pages at
#    /admin/applications, /admin/plans, /admin/settings and
#    /admin/license-codes — the exact same paths as billing's controllers.
#    Without /api those collide and Caddy can only pick one upstream: route
#    to app and the admin pages go blank, route to Next.js and the API dies.
#    /api is the namespace boundary between UI and API, not leftover cruft.
#
# 2. The per-service segment IS gone (was /api/iam/*, /api/scheduling/*, ...
#    reproducing gateway's old StripPrefix=2). Those named services stopped
#    existing at the modulith collapse — ADR-008. app is one process whose
#    modules own disjoint bare path roots (identity's AuthController maps
#    /auth, enrollment's ClassController maps /organizations/..., billing's
#    OrderController maps /orders), so a single strip is all that is needed.
#
# handle_path strips the matched /api prefix before proxying, which is why
# every controller in pte-api maps a bare root and never writes /api itself.
(api_routes) {
	handle_path /api/* {
		reverse_proxy app:8091
	}
}
